WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.957
In the early Tang dynasty, the eminent monk Xuanzang saw that the Buddhist scriptures had not been translated accurately enough, and resolved to go to India himself to seek the true scriptures.

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:13.771
Xuanzang carried simple luggage and prepared to travel a very long way.

00:00:14.121 --> 00:00:21.513
In the year 627, he secretly left Chang'an — one man and one horse — and headed west.

00:00:21.863 --> 00:00:29.255
He crossed the vast desert, running out of water and food, and nearly died on the road several times.

00:00:29.605 --> 00:00:35.245
He pressed on through the desert and never gave up.

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:43.251
Seventeen years later, Xuanzang returned to Chang'an with more than six hundred Buddhist scriptures and was warmly welcomed.

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:47.465
Crowds filled the area before the city gate, all come to welcome him back.

00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:58.663
He spent his remaining years translating scriptures and wrote the Great Tang Records on the Western Regions. The later novel Journey to the West was inspired by his story.

